"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,175\nThe Learning Brain. Series 7: Digital Learning\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,225\nEpisode 8\n\n3\n00:00:07,305 --> 00:00:09,557\nThe laptop next to you\n\n4\n00:00:12,357 --> 00:00:15,036\nA student opens a laptop to take notes,\n\n5\n00:00:15,189 --> 00:00:16,218\nthen a browser tab,\n\n6\n00:00:16,238 --> 00:00:17,088\nthen another.\n\n7\n00:00:17,427 --> 00:00:18,705\nWho pays the price?\n\n8\n00:00:18,895 --> 00:00:20,281\nNot only that student.\n\n9\n00:00:20,451 --> 00:00:23,230\nIn 2013, Faria Sana,\n\n10\n00:00:23,506 --> 00:00:27,240\nTina Weston and Nicholas Cepeda ran a simulated lecture.\n\n11\n00:00:27,798 --> 00:00:32,538\nStudents who multitasked on a laptop scored lower on a comprehension test.\n\n12\n00:00:32,951 --> 00:00:39,868\nMore surprising: students seated where they could see a multitasking classmate's screen also scored lower,\n\n13\n00:00:40,173 --> 00:00:43,141\neven though they were not multitasking themselves.\n\n14\n00:00:43,311 --> 00:00:46,203\nA larger field experiment came from West Point.\n\n15\n00:00:46,793 --> 00:00:52,927\nSusan Payne Carter and colleagues randomly assigned fifty classrooms of an economics course,\n\n16\n00:00:53,177 --> 00:00:55,341\nwith seven hundred twenty six students,\n\n17\n00:00:55,618 --> 00:00:57,872\nto allow laptops and tablets,\n\n18\n00:00:58,143 --> 00:01:00,572\nto allow only tablets lying flat,\n\n19\n00:01:00,831 --> 00:01:02,614\nor to ban devices.\n\n20\n00:01:02,784 --> 00:01:04,120\nOn the final exam,\n\n21\n00:01:04,366 --> 00:01:12,983\nstudents in classrooms that allowed devices scored zero point one eight standard deviations lower than those in device free rooms.\n\n22\n00:01:13,402 --> 00:01:16,011\nRestricting to flat tablets barely helped.\n\n23\n00:01:16,439 --> 00:01:20,385\nNo subgroup of students benefited from having devices available.\n\n24\n00:01:20,555 --> 00:01:21,531\nSome caution.\n\n25\n00:01:21,896 --> 00:01:27,184\nThe authors were careful: they did not claim all classroom computer use is harmful.\n\n26\n00:01:27,563 --> 00:01:30,942\nExercises that use devices on purpose may help.\n\n27\n00:01:31,425 --> 00:01:34,697\nThe problem is open ended access during lecture.\n\n28\n00:01:34,867 --> 00:01:38,614\nThis fits what we saw in Series four about notifications,\n\n29\n00:01:38,852 --> 00:01:41,539\nand in Series six about multitasking.\n\n30\n00:01:42,077 --> 00:01:45,170\nAttention is limited, and screens compete for it,\n\n31\n00:01:45,356 --> 00:01:47,404\nincluding the screens of others.\n\n32\n00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:48,319\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:48,699 --> 00:01:50,961\nSet clear device norms on the first day,\n\n34\n00:01:50,981 --> 00:01:52,531\nand explain the evidence.\n\n35\n00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:54,470\nUse devices on purpose,\n\n36\n00:01:54,490 --> 00:01:56,158\nfor polls or problems,\n\n37\n00:01:56,232 --> 00:01:57,277\nthen put them away.\n\n38\n00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:00,663\nConsider a section of the room for laptop users,\n\n39\n00:02:00,683 --> 00:02:03,080\nso screens are not in everyone's view.\n\n40\n00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:04,986\nProtect accommodations.\n\n41\n00:02:05,344 --> 00:02:09,841\nOne tip to share with students: sit where you can't see other screens.\n\n42\n00:02:10,011 --> 00:02:12,016\nAttention is shared in a classroom.\n\n43\n00:02:12,232 --> 00:02:13,664\nSo are distractions.\n\n"